WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.850
Film Courage: Harvey, once you say “Yes”
to a project, what are some of the first steps?

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:08.970
Harvey Mason, Jr.: When I take on a project,
I start researching it, looking back at what

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:13.840
they’ve done before or reading any kind
of background material, scripts, outlines,

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:14.840
things like that.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:17.789
If it’s an artist, I’m listening to all
of the music that they have recorded, what

00:00:17.789 --> 00:00:21.419
the voices are, listening to who maybe the
actors are.

00:00:21.419 --> 00:00:25.689
And in the case of some of these movies, you’re
looking back at the history of the music that

00:00:25.689 --> 00:00:30.050
is going to be featured in the film and kind
of seeing…Okay, what can I do to these songs

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:32.029
to make them contemporary and relevant.

00:00:32.029 --> 00:00:34.160
How can I push them?

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:37.370
How careful do I have to be because there
is some classic copyrights there that you

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:39.100
don’t want to tamper with?

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:43.370
So you start researching it and then you start
laying out an organizational plan on how you

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:45.780
are going to attack the project.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.980
The latest film I am working on had about
70 songs in it.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:53.150
So you have to be a little bit strategic with
how you do it.

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:57.690
My next project is kind of broken out into
three different groups of styles of songs

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.180
so I kind of start organizing it.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:04.960
The next step is to start casting and thinking
about who am I going to use…the director’s

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:06.240
casting the film.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:07.429
I am casting the music.

00:01:07.429 --> 00:01:11.930
Who am I going to use to create the right
sound for each individual piece of music?

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:16.539
And luckily living in Los Angeles, we have
access to the best musicians and best vocalists,

00:01:16.539 --> 00:01:20.259
so I can pull from an amazing group of talent.

00:01:20.259 --> 00:01:27.770
But part of my job is to specifically make
sure that the people that I bring in accomplish

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:30.520
the sound that I want for that particular
piece of music.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:33.270
Film Courage: Okay…let’s talk about SING
(movie) then.

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:38.100
So once they presented you with the script
and you knew who some of the voice actors

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:42.030
were, how did you fit the different songs
for each character?

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:47.020
Harvey Mason, Jr.: Well that was a trick because
there was so much material and so many characters

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:51.681
but ultimately what the job was for me was
to take kind of the playlist that they had

00:01:51.681 --> 00:01:56.939
put together and figure out what was the personality
of each character and how can I translate

00:01:56.939 --> 00:02:01.100
that into kind of a sonic, genre specific
sound for them.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:03.759
So Ash (Scarlett Johansson’s character)
the porcupine is real edgy, rocky.

00:02:03.759 --> 00:02:07.990
So we just knew the songs that she was going
to do just had to have that edge.

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:16.269
Reese’s character is kind of pop, a cleaner-cut
pop artist.

00:02:16.269 --> 00:02:22.060
So really it was about recognizing the personality
of the artist and then figuring out which

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:26.510
songs worked for them and then ultimately
making sure that I gave them that consistent

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:27.510
sound.

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:32.239
Film Courage: Now once you decided which artist
you were going to use, what is your process?

00:02:32.239 --> 00:02:37.569
Are you contacting the song writer or the
artist themselves who performed it?

00:02:37.569 --> 00:02:39.480
What is the process like in getting approval?

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:44.939
Harvey Mason, Jr.: Once the songs were chosen
and approved by the heads of the studio, there

00:02:44.939 --> 00:02:47.019
is another person who works with us.

00:02:47.019 --> 00:02:48.510
His name is Jojo Villanueva.

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:50.330
He’s the music supervisor.

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:54.630
So his job is specifically to go in and license
and clear all of these songs.

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:57.310
I think he cleared almost 100 pieces of music.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:01.650
So that process involves going to the publisher,
going to the artists’ record companies,

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:05.530
the performers, all those people, depending
on if we’re going to use the actual master

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:08.950
recording or if we’re going to recreate
it, there are a bunch of different variables

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:09.950
there.

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:14.110
But he has to reach out and negotiated all
those fees and prices and usage and that’s

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:15.490
a nutty job.

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:19.990
He’s been doing that for SING movie for
almost two years.

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:21.420
That’s a big job!

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:22.489
Film Courage: Wow!

00:03:22.489 --> 00:03:26.000
Were there some songs that you presented and
thought “You know this would be a great

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:27.000
match!”

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:30.520
I know you had Elton John, Stevie Wonder,
Leonard Cohen (songs) in the movie.

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:34.170
But what if other people didn’t think it
was a match?

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:38.330
And what is your process for proving your
point or working with them (possibly just

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:40.890
turning over the control to what they want).

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:41.890
What was this like?

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:45.450
Harvey Mason, Jr.: Well to be honest, I didn't
choose a lot of the songs.

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:49.890
I added my opinion and there are a few songs
that I brought to the table, but a lot of

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:51.540
the songs are chosen by the director.

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:55.409
And so because he chose those songs there
wasn’t a lot of people we had to prove it

00:03:55.409 --> 00:03:56.409
to.

00:03:56.409 --> 00:04:00.799
Maybe Chris Meledandri, the head of the studio,
but for the most part it was really knowing

00:04:00.799 --> 00:04:07.190
the songs he wanted and then how do we make
those songs work in the context of the film

00:04:07.190 --> 00:04:09.060
and that was the position I was in.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:12.780
I mean Garth loves this song but he doesn’t
know how to piece it together, how it’s

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:13.780
going to fit.

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:15.709
He’s not even sure that it is.

00:04:15.709 --> 00:04:18.630
If anything I am almost taking his idea and
trying to prove it back.

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:19.630
I’m like “Yeah, Garth.

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:20.820
That’s a great song title.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:22.540
Let’s figure out how we can flip it.”

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:28.650
In other cases like the grand finale songs,
there was some uncertainty about those songs.

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:29.650
We went back and forth.

00:04:29.650 --> 00:04:31.610
We tried three or four different songs in
different spots.

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:33.449
We would do little rough demos.

00:04:33.449 --> 00:04:37.330
Don’t You Worry About a Thing was one in
particular where we did a lot of versions

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:39.270
of and improvements too.

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:45.590
We started in one place, we started here and
ended up way over here on that song.

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:50.120
Ultimately you’re just trying to create
the right emotional feel, to hit the right

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:55.620
beats for the director and the story with
the music so you’re choosing the songs based

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:56.620
on that.

00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:59.460
But once they’re chosen you can take a song
ten different ways in production.

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:04.139
It’s all how you produce it: the tempo,
the key, the instrumentation.

00:05:04.139 --> 00:05:09.330
So as long as I was aware of what the song
needed to accomplish, I was able to take those

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:12.220
songs and manipulate them to kind of fit the
scene.

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:13.220
Question for the Viewers: How is your process
of incorporating music into a movie the same

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:13.222
or different?

